      "text": "First, we need to identify the conditions under which both the number \\( ab \\) and the digits \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are divisible by 3. A number is divisible by 3 if the sum of its digits is divisible by 3. The product \\( ab \\) must also be divisible by 3.\n\n### Step 1: Digits \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) divisible by 3\nThe numbers from 1 to 6 are: \\( 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 \\). We need to identify which of these numbers are divisible by 3:\n- \\( 3 \\) and \\( 6 \\) are divisible by 3.\n\n### Step 2: \\( ab \\) divisible by 3\nThe product \\( ab \\) must be divisible by 3. The possible pairs \\((a, b)\\) are those where at least one of \\( a \\) or \\( b \\) is 3 or 6. We will consider each case separately.\n\n#### Case 1: \\( a = 3 \\) or \\( a = 6 \\)\n- If \\( a = 3 \\), then \\( b \\) can be any of \\( 1, 2, 4, 5, 6 \\) (5 choices).\n- If \\( a = 6 \\), then \\( b \\) can be any of \\( 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 \\) (5 choices).\n\nThus, there are \\( 5 + 5 = 10 \\) pairs where \\( a = 3 \\) or \\( a = 6 \\).\n\n#### Case 2: \\( b = 3 \\) or \\( b = 6 \\)\n- If \\( b = 3 \\), then \\( a \\) can be any of \\( 1, 2, 4, 5, 6 \\) (5 choices).\n- If \\( b = 6 \\), then \\( a \\) can be any of \\( 1, 2, 4, 5 \\) (4 choices).\n\nThus, there are \\( 5 + 4 = 9 \\) pairs where \\( b = 3 \\) or \\( b = 6 \\).\n\n### Step 3: Total number of favorable pairs\nAdding the favorable pairs from both cases, we get:\n\\[ 10 + 9 = 19 \\]\n\n### Step 4: Total number of possible outcomes\nSince each die roll has 6 possible outcomes, the total number of possible outcomes when rolling two dice is:\n\\[ 6 \\times 6 = 36 \\]\n\n### Step 5: Probability calculation\nThe probability that \\( ab \\) and \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are all divisible by 3 is the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes to the total number of possible outcomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{19}{36}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{19}{36}}\n\\]",
